{
  "id": "bdb7d794-5eb2-41f5-823f-5f4f719266a3",
  "kind": "Theorem",
  "definition_keys": [],
  "heading": null,
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{Algebraic Laws of Complex Conjugation}",
  "body_text": null,
  "proof_text": "Let $z = x_1 + i y_1$ and $w = x_2 + i y_2$ with $x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n1. **Additivity**: We compute:\n   $$ z + w = (x_1 + x_2) + i(y_1 + y_2), $$\n   so by definition of conjugation:\n   $$ \\overline{z + w} = (x_1 + x_2) - i(y_1 + y_2) = (x_1 - i y_1) + (x_2 - i y_2) = \\overline{z} + \\overline{w}. $$\n\n2. **Multiplicativity**: By definition of complex multiplication:\n   $$ zw = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) + i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n   Its conjugate is:\n   $$ \\overline{zw} = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n   On the other hand, expanding $\\overline{z} \\cdot \\overline{w}$:\n   $$ (x_1 - i y_1)(x_2 - i y_2) = (x_1 x_2 + (-y_1)(-y_2)i^2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1) = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n   Thus $\\overline{zw} = \\overline{z} \\cdot \\overline{w}$.\n\n3. **Product with Conjugate**: Computing directly:\n   $$ z \\overline{z} = (x_1 + i y_1)(x_1 - i y_1) = x_1^2 - (i y_1)^2 = x_1^2 + y_1^2 = |z|^2. $$\n\n4. **Multiplicative Inverse**: If $z \\ne 0$, then $|z|^2 > 0$ is a non-zero real number.\n   Dividing the identity $z \\overline{z} = |z|^2$ by $z |z|^2$ yields:\n   $$ z^{-1} = \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{\\overline{z}}{|z|^2}. $$",
  "proof_source": "Proof: \"\"\"\n  Let $z = x_1 + i y_1$ and $w = x_2 + i y_2$ with $x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n  1. **Additivity**: We compute:\n     $$ z + w = (x_1 + x_2) + i(y_1 + y_2), $$\n     so by definition of conjugation:\n     $$ \\overline{z + w} = (x_1 + x_2) - i(y_1 + y_2) = (x_1 - i y_1) + (x_2 - i y_2) = \\overline{z} + \\overline{w}. $$\n\n  2. **Multiplicativity**: By definition of complex multiplication:\n     $$ zw = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) + i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n     Its conjugate is:\n     $$ \\overline{zw} = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n     On the other hand, expanding $\\overline{z} \\cdot \\overline{w}$:\n     $$ (x_1 - i y_1)(x_2 - i y_2) = (x_1 x_2 + (-y_1)(-y_2)i^2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1) = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n     Thus $\\overline{zw} = \\overline{z} \\cdot \\overline{w}$.\n\n  3. **Product with Conjugate**: Computing directly:\n     $$ z \\overline{z} = (x_1 + i y_1)(x_1 - i y_1) = x_1^2 - (i y_1)^2 = x_1^2 + y_1^2 = |z|^2. $$\n\n  4. **Multiplicative Inverse**: If $z \\ne 0$, then $|z|^2 > 0$ is a non-zero real number.\n     Dividing the identity $z \\overline{z} = |z|^2$ by $z |z|^2$ yields:\n     $$ z^{-1} = \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{\\overline{z}}{|z|^2}. $$\n\"\"\"",
  "page": null,
  "source": "Theorem:\ngiven: z, w is \\complex\nthen:\n. \\complex.conjugate(z \\.complex.+./ w) = \\complex.conjugate(z) \\.complex.+./ \\complex.conjugate(w)\n. \\complex.conjugate(z \\.complex.*./ w) = \\complex.conjugate(z) \\.complex.*./ \\complex.conjugate(w)\n. z \\.complex.*./ \\complex.conjugate(z) = \\complex.modulus(z) \\.real.*./ \\complex.modulus(z)\n. if: z != \\complex.zero\n  then: \\complex.one \\.complex.div./ z = \\complex.conjugate(z) \\.complex.div./ (\\complex.modulus(z) \\.real.*./ \\complex.modulus(z))\nDocumented:\n. called: \"Algebraic Laws of Complex Conjugation\"\n. description: \"Conjugation preserves addition and multiplication, and its\n                product with the original number is the squared modulus.\"\nProof: \"\"\"\n  Let $z = x_1 + i y_1$ and $w = x_2 + i y_2$ with $x_1, x_2, y_1, y_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n  1. **Additivity**: We compute:\n     $$ z + w = (x_1 + x_2) + i(y_1 + y_2), $$\n     so by definition of conjugation:\n     $$ \\overline{z + w} = (x_1 + x_2) - i(y_1 + y_2) = (x_1 - i y_1) + (x_2 - i y_2) = \\overline{z} + \\overline{w}. $$\n\n  2. **Multiplicativity**: By definition of complex multiplication:\n     $$ zw = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) + i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n     Its conjugate is:\n     $$ \\overline{zw} = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n     On the other hand, expanding $\\overline{z} \\cdot \\overline{w}$:\n     $$ (x_1 - i y_1)(x_2 - i y_2) = (x_1 x_2 + (-y_1)(-y_2)i^2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1) = (x_1 x_2 - y_1 y_2) - i(x_1 y_2 + x_2 y_1). $$\n     Thus $\\overline{zw} = \\overline{z} \\cdot \\overline{w}$.\n\n  3. **Product with Conjugate**: Computing directly:\n     $$ z \\overline{z} = (x_1 + i y_1)(x_1 - i y_1) = x_1^2 - (i y_1)^2 = x_1^2 + y_1^2 = |z|^2. $$\n\n  4. **Multiplicative Inverse**: If $z \\ne 0$, then $|z|^2 > 0$ is a non-zero real number.\n     Dividing the identity $z \\overline{z} = |z|^2$ by $z |z|^2$ yields:\n     $$ z^{-1} = \\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{\\overline{z}}{|z|^2}. $$\n\"\"\"\nId: \"bdb7d794-5eb2-41f5-823f-5f4f719266a3\"",
